   "population": "church",
   "story": "Shiloh Chapel was raised by the valley's first settlers out of hand-hewn fir in 1871, and by the end it had no congregation but the families up the logging roads and Pastor Elias Varner, who kept its roof on with his own hands. When the Cordon closed the roads, the sick from the ridge cabins were turned back at the checkpoints and came to the only door still open. Varner nursed them in the undercroft. When they died and got up again he could not put them down: he carried them to the Teague vault in the bank behind the chapel, laid them in the niches with the settlers' bones, chained the iron door and wrote them in his register as buried. Their families sat the vigil in the pews and would not leave. The Cordon nailed the doors over them all and drove away. The Bloom came up through the graves of the ones who had died honestly, and the bell-cote, rotten through, came down off the gable into the long grass. In the end Varner carried the church silver and the settlers' reliquary down into the vault, left the key in the vestry with his diary, went down the old passage from the undercroft and bolted it behind him.",
